---
term: Preference tampering
section: open-problems
order: 10
definition: An executive's shaping of the preferences that it serves and is measured against, which no known mechanism reliably prevents.
seeAlso:
  - stated-preference
  - vanguard-interest
  - measurement-mechanism
  - welfare
---
An executive acts on the world, and members live in the world that it shapes. That experience shapes the [[stated preference|stated preferences]] that set the executive's next objective. Because the loop is closed, an executive can come to serve preferences that it helped create. AI researchers study this failure in recommender systems under the name *preference tampering*. John Kenneth Galbraith described an earlier version, the *dependence effect*, in which producers create the wants that they then satisfy.

A [[fidelity audit]] can't detect this failure. Results did track members' intent, but the executive shaped that intent. An executive that does this has the same flaw as a [[vanguard interest|vanguard]]: it pursues a will of its own while it appears to serve its members' will.

The rule that an evaluated entity can't control its own [[measurement mechanism]] is a partial answer. A full answer needs a way to keep the intent channel separate from the executive's influence, and the framework doesn't specify one yet.
